now international talks on iran's  nuclear programme have resumed after a  five-month hiatus the meetings in vienna  include representatives from china  germany france britain and russia the  talks are meant to bring iran back into  compliance with a deal it signed in 2015  that's supposed to ensure it doesn't  acquire a nuclear weapon and the u.s  abandoned that deal three years later  under the trump administration iran then  went on to step up its enrichment of  uranium and limited access and limited  access to international inspectors but  now the parties are trying to update the  agreement with the us participating  indirectly  executive vice president of the  institute for peace and diplomacy in  toronto canada we asked him earlier what  chance of success these negotiations  have  i don't think it's the last chance in  complex negotiations like these can take  a while and this was the case even with  the previous or initial agreement  known as the joint comprehensive plan of  action and look today was just the first  day of negotiations and it's just too  early to make an assessment whether  it's this is go there's going to be a  deal reached or not but i see positive  signs and signals coming out of vienna  and this makes me optimistic about the  prospect of iran nuclear talks and let  me tell you why i am optimistic today  more than i was weeks ago before the  negotiations restarted first iran has  agreed to continue the negotiations  where they were left off with the  previous administration this means that  there's not going to be more time spent  nor dedicated on renegotiating what was  already negotiated negotiated with the  previous government and it means that  now there is a foundation up in which  that more issues could be discussed and  potentially resolved by other parties to  the nuclear deal and another positive  development is the fact that two working  groups one specifically on sanction  relief and another one on  iran's nuclear activities will be  restarting their work in order to breach  the gap between the demands and  expectations between tehran and  washington
 

